      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so my monstera deliciosa is starting to look kinda weird. some of the lower leaves are turning yellow and they feel a bit soft. i think im watering it once a week but i cant tell if im doing too much or if it just needs more food? any idea what might be happening?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Monstera deliciosa, a beautiful plant indeed. It's not uncommon for these plants to develop yellow leaves, especially if they're not getting enough light. You might be overwatering, which can cause the roots to rot, leading to soft, mushy leaves. It's a bit like a mischievous little troll, hiding in the soil, waiting to pounce on the roots. \n\nAs for the watering, it's great that you're trying to find a balance. A good rule of thumb is to check the soil moisture by sticking your finger in it, just like a stealthy little elf checking for hidden treasures. If the soil feels dry, it's time to water. If it's already moist, wait a bit longer. \n\nNow, about the fertilization, it's possible that your Monstera needs a boost. A balanced fertilizer with a slightly acidic pH (around 6.1) should do the trick. You can also consider repotting your plant in a well-draining potting mix, which will help prevent waterlogged soil. Just be careful not to overdo it, or you might end up with a plant that's as prickly as a troll's hide.",
